"Nobody in ancient times called these routes the “Silk Road.” The German geographer Ferdinand von Richthofen used the name in 1877, though earlier German writers had used it too, and it spread widely only in the 1900s. UNESCO describes the Silk Roads as a network of land and sea routes, used for well over 1,500 years, along which silk and many other goods were exchanged. On land, the routes linked China with Central Asia, Persia, and the Mediterranean. Around 138 BCE the Han envoy Zhang Qian set out west, and the Han set up commanderies along the Hexi Corridor, including Dunhuang. Travelers had to cross deserts, mountains, and the lands of many different kingdoms, so caravans moved from oasis to oasis. Few people traveled the whole way. Britannica puts it simply: goods were handled stage by stage by middlemen. Sogdian merchants from around Samarkand built a network of partners in many towns; letters they wrote around 313 CE mention silver, linen, musk, and pepper. In 97 CE a Han envoy, Gan Ying, reached the sea in the west and turned back, after sailors there warned him the crossing could take years. The sea routes were just as important. Ships sailed between China, Southeast Asia, India, the Persian Gulf, the Red Sea, and East Africa, timing their voyages to the monsoon winds. A ship that sank near the island of Belitung around the 820s or 830s carried about 60,000 objects from China, most of them painted bowls. Later, ports such as Quanzhou in China and Kilwa in East Africa grew rich on this trade. Silk was only one of many things that traveled. Horses, grapes, and alfalfa came east to China; paper and the skill of making it went west, reaching Samarkand by 751 and Baghdad by 793. Glass, spices, porcelain, and coins moved too. So did beliefs: Buddhism, Manichaeism, Christianity, and Islam all spread along these routes. And so did disease: the Black Death of the 1340s probably traveled along these routes, though scholars still debate exactly how. No single people controlled the routes. Chinese, Sogdian, Persian, Indian, Arab, Malay, Swahili, and many other traders each knew their own stretch. What made the network work was not one long road, but many shorter connections, each linking one place to the next. That is why ideas and goods could travel so far, even though most people did not.": "2a45a7d432b9",
"On land, the routes linked China with Central Asia, Persia, and the Mediterranean. Around 138 BCE the Han envoy Zhang Qian set out west, and the Han set up commanderies along the Hexi Corridor, including Dunhuang. Travelers had to cross deserts, mountains, and the lands of many different kingdoms, so caravans moved from oasis to oasis.": "14d8650d3105",
