"Silk was only one of many things that traveled. Horses, grapes, and alfalfa came east to China; paper and the skill of making it went west, reaching Samarkand by 751 and Baghdad by 793. Glass, spices, porcelain, and coins moved too. So did beliefs: Buddhism, Manichaeism, Christianity, and Islam all spread along these routes. And so did disease: the Black Death of the 1340s probably traveled along these routes, though scholars still debate exactly how.": "8b698b5cc8bd",
